    "body": "I apologise if this has been discussed before; I could not find an issue for it.\r\n\r\nCurrently the spec specifically refers to users who have produced/uploaded the burrito, by name and by unique identifier.  I am not really a fan of this, for several reasons, but willing to be convinced otherwise.\r\n\r\nFirstly, including personally identifiable information may mean that burritos are affected by data protection regulations such as GDPR, which may place legal limits on the ability to transfer burritos to servers in other countries.  IANAL, so I do not know how far-reaching the implications are (and I might be completely wrong on this).\r\n\r\nSecondly, from a purely conceptual level I am not sure that it should be considered in-scope for the burrito metadata to say something that is not descriptive of the contents but rather of the person who created it.  Including the name and version of the software that generated it is useful because it may say something meaningful about the contents thereof, but the identity of the person who did it does not.  (And when burritos are offered for wider distribution, people who publish burritos on behalf of their organisation may not even want their personal names attached.)\r\n\r\nThirdly, I can't see how it's *useful*.  The fact that identifiers are system-specific, and privacy considerations mean that user information cannot just be queried by an arbitrary party from a public API without the user's consent, effectively means that a system is likely storing this purely for its own consumption, but a system that needs this will likely already be storing its own event log accompanying the actual metadata.  But, this is purely my own speculation, so it would be valuable for others to weigh in.",
